    <!-- Fact Popups -->
    <string name="fact_popup_title">Connectome Discovery</string>
    <string name="btn_dismiss">Got it</string>

    <string name="fact_motor_feed">MN9 is a motor neuron that extends the fly\'s proboscis to eat. In a published whole-brain model, sugar-sensing neurons at 100 Hz drove it to about 80 percent of its maximum firing.</string>
    <string name="fact_motor_escape">DNp01 is the Giant Fiber, a pair of descending neurons at the heart of the fly\'s escape circuit. There is one on each side of the brain.</string>
    <string name="fact_sensory_loom">In this dataset, LPLC2 and LC4 cells provide the most synapses onto the Giant Fibers among visual cells. They detect objects growing larger, like a hand coming in fast.</string>
    <string name="fact_reward_pam">About 120 to 130 PAM neurons release dopamine onto the mushroom body, the fly\'s learning center, and signal reward.</string>
    <string name="fact_sensory_reel">In this subgraph the strongest route to the PAM neurons comes from visual relay cells, not from the feeding pathway. Doomscrolling wins the dopamine race in this wiring.</string>
    <string name="fact_sensory_sugar">These 20 sugar-sensing taste neurons are real cells from the FlyWire connectome, which maps 139,255 neurons and about 50 million synapses.</string>
    <string name="fact_sensory_jon">Johnston\'s organ neurons sit in the antennae and sense movement. In a published whole-brain model, activating them predicted firing in a grooming circuit, so rubbing the fly in the bath makes it groom.</string>
